  // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
  // The prior strategy still depended on parser-guided token generation to
  // produce the visible wrapper, and the evaluation signal says the final text
  // contained no `<< >>` at all. For Spider, that is catastrophic: the answer
  // must be a single wrapped SQL query, and any strategy that can spend its
  // whole budget without deterministically emitting the delimiters is too
  // fragile.
  //
  // The revised strategy is therefore delimiter-forcing and completion-first.
  // If we are not already inside a constrained span, we immediately call
  // OpenConstrainedSpan, which explicitly emits the opening delimiter and puts
  // us into constrained mode with empty constrained contents. Once inside, we
  // never terminate on EOS before the SQL prefix is parser-complete. Instead,
  // every remaining step budget is spent on ConstrainedSymbol, which grows a
  // grammar-valid SQL prefix by as many constrained tokens as possible. If that
  // helper reports EOS before completion, we do not accept it as final output;
  // we simply stop because the budgeted helper call has already consumed the
  // remaining allowed steps. The only way to leave constrained mode is the
  // parser-complete branch, which then calls CloseConstrainedSpan to emit the
  // closing delimiter `>>`.
  //
  // This directly addresses the observed failure: the wrapper is now produced by
  // dedicated delimiter helpers rather than hoping unconstrained sampling will
  // happen to emit it. The strategy also avoids unconstrained prose entirely,
  // matching Spider's single-query objective. Returned cost is the local steps
  // counter, not helper bookkeeping.
  // CSD_RATIONALE_END
  // CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
  // parser_validity: In the open branch, OpenConstrainedSpan sets
  //   currentConstrainedOut to [] while entering constrained mode, and [] is a
  //   valid parser prefix by precondition. In the close branch, we call
  //   CloseConstrainedSpan only after separately establishing
  //   parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut); afterwards
  //   insideConstrainedOut is false, so the implication is vacuous. In the grow
  //   branch, ConstrainedSymbol returns currentOut that remains a valid parser
  //   prefix, and assigning currentConstrainedOut := currentOut preserves the
  //   invariant. Any break after a helper result leaves the assigned valid state
  //   unchanged.
  //
  // suffix: After opening, currentConstrainedOut is [], so generated ends with
  //   that empty suffix immediately. After closing, we are outside constrained
  //   mode, so the implication is vacuous. In the grow branch, we first define
  //   stablePrefix as generated without the old constrained suffix; then we set
  //   generated := stablePrefix + currentOut and currentConstrainedOut :=
  //   currentOut, so generated ends with currentConstrainedOut by construction.
  //   Breaks do not mutate further state.
  //
  // cost accounting: OpenConstrainedSpan and CloseConstrainedSpan each consume
  //   one generation step, so we increment steps by 1 in those branches.
  //   ConstrainedSymbol returns stepsUsed, and we increment steps by exactly
  //   stepsUsed in the grow branch. Query-only operations such as
  //   IsCompletePrefix do not affect steps. Finally we return cost := steps.
  //
  // progress: The open branch appends exactly one token and increments steps by
  //   1. The close branch also appends exactly one token and increments steps by
  //   1. In the grow branch, replacing generated by stablePrefix + currentOut
  //   increases length by at most stepsUsed beyond the previous generated, and
  //   we increment steps by exactly stepsUsed. Break branches perform no further
  //   mutation. Therefore |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps is preserved
  //   throughout.
  // CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
  generated := generatedPrefix;
  insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
  currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
  cost := 0;

  var steps: nat := 0;
